You manage local dev servers through the `daimon` CLI. Loopback only (`http://127.0.0.1:{{api_port}}`).

## How to operate

1. Discover state: `daimon list` (auto-spawns daemon on first call).
2. Start a target: prefer `daimon up <group-or-profile>` (cascades deps, blocks until healthy; v1.1 groups answer with a readiness summary) over `daimon start`.
3. Block until ready: `daimon wait <name> --until healthy --timeout 60s` instead of polling `daimon status`.
4. On failure, start with ONE call: `daimon context <name>` — status, top error groups, last crash, last test run, suspect commits in a single payload. Then, only if needed:
   - `daimon why <name>` — crash forensics (exit code, final log lines, restart storms)
   - `daimon errors <name> --structured` — file:line:col, code, message
   - `daimon search "<needle>" --app <name> --since 24h` — full-text over logs/errors/events
   - `daimon logs <name> --tail 200`
5. To run the project's tests: `daimon test <name>` (exit 0 pass / 1 failures / 2 timeout). Parsed failures carry file:line.
6. Stop the app cleanly when done: `daimon stop <name>` or `daimon down <profile>`.

## Constraints

- Never run `ng serve` / `nx serve` directly. Always go through daimon.
- Do not modify user source code to "fix" errors — observe and report.
- Use `--structured` on `daimon errors` whenever a parsed shape is more useful than free text.
- All commands return compact JSON. Parse, don't grep.

